{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>When YHWH your Elohim enlarges your border, and you say, 'I will eat meat.'</b> This teaches that a person does not pant after desires except from within excessive expansion, and 'a lion does not roar except from within a basket of meat' (Berakhot 32). Therefore it says, 'When YHWH enlarges your border' — and this will bring you to the point of removing the veil of shame from your face, until you say with a full mouth, 'I will eat meat.' And this is somewhat like casting off the yoke of the Kingdom of Heaven and investigating which is the place of sacrifices, and the cause of all this is 'when the place that YHWH will choose is far from you,' for whoever is nearer and nearer to the sanctuary of YHWH has upon himself the fear of the Kingdom of Heaven all the more, as it is written: ״וּמִקְדָּשִׁי תִּירָאוּ״ (Leviticus 19:30) — which means, from the sanctuary it is drawn down that the fear of the Kingdom of Heaven will be upon you. However, 'when the place is far from you' causes YHWH's presence to be far from your innards; therefore all day long you will crave a craving, and you will not be ashamed to say, 'I will eat meat.' I permit the matter to you: ״וְזָבַחְתָּ מִבְּקָרְךָ וְגוֹ׳ כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוִּיתִךָ״ — not at all times, but only occasionally, at the time of the craving's surge. And this is what it says: ״אַךְ כַּאֲשֶׁר יֵאָכֵל אֶת הַצְּבִי וְאֶת הָאַיָּל כֵּן תֹּאכְלֶנּוּ״. And as it is stated (Deuteronomy 12:15): ״כִּי יָצוּד צֵיד חַיָּה אוֹ עוֹף״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ḥullin 84a): the Torah teaches you derech eretz, that a person should not eat meat except on this occasion." "The explanation of the matter is that if a person accustoms himself to eat from the domesticated animals found with him at home — ox or sheep or goat — then all day long he will crave a craving and accustom himself to eating it a daily thing, day by day. But if he does not eat until he hunts in forests and wildernesses game of wild animal or bird, which entails danger and great toil to hunt them, then his craving will subside — for the eating is not worth the magnitude of the pain and effort. For it was not for nothing that Isaac said to Esau, ״וְצֵא הַשָּׂדֶה וְצוּדָה לִּי צָיִד״ (Genesis 27:3) — did he not have at home something already hunted? And why did he agitate him with all this agitation to move about in places of danger? Rather, he conducted himself according to the derech eretz that the Torah taught: that one should not eat meat except on this occasion, specifically by hunting, so that he would not become habituated to it. Therefore it says: ״אַךְ כַּאֲשֶׁר יֵאָכֵל אֶת הַצְּבִי וְאֶת הָאַיָּל כֵּן תֹּאכְלֶנּוּ״ — that is to say, only on this condition, that he not be habituated to it, and that he eat it as one eats the deer and the gazelle, which presumably he is not habituated to, since they are wild animals and require hunting, and on account of the effort he reduces his eating of them — so too he should eat even ordinary meat. And this interpretation is precious, and settles the meaning of the word 'אַךְ' better than Rashi's interpretation." "And since we find that in anything hunted from ownerless places there is heightened grounds to suspect that it might be stolen property — for this reason Isaac warned Esau to bring him from the ownerless and not from stolen goods, because perhaps what is hunted had owners and fled from them — therefore it is stated here: ״וְזָבַחְתָּ מִבְּקָרְךָ וּמִצֹּאנְךָ אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לְךָ ה׳״. Now does it enter your mind that he would slaughter from those which YHWH did not give him? Rather, the intent is that he should eat specifically on this occasion, ״כִּי יָצוּד צֵיד חַיָּה אוֹ עוֹף״, and there is reason to suspect it for theft as stated; therefore it says, ״אֲשֶׁר נָתַן לְךָ ה׳״ — that even in your hunted game, to exclude stolen property, what is not from YHWH. And concerning this it says: ״כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוִּיתִךָ״ — and I warned you about theft, or I warned you not to eat meat except on this occasion."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:52.432683277Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}